1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,777
Dolphins sleep with one eye open.

2
00:00:01,797 --> 00:00:02,995
Literally.

3
00:00:03,015 --> 00:00:04,389
Half their brain sleeps while

4
00:00:04,389 --> 00:00:05,899
the other half stays awake.

5
00:00:05,919 --> 00:00:06,810
Scientists call it

6
00:00:06,810 --> 00:00:08,140
unihemispheric sleep.

7
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:09,412
And nearly all the wiring

8
00:00:09,412 --> 00:00:10,624
from each eye crosses to

9
00:00:10,624 --> 00:00:12,281
the opposite half of the brain.

10
00:00:12,301 --> 00:00:13,153
So when the left half

11
00:00:13,153 --> 00:00:13,871
is asleep,

12
00:00:13,891 --> 00:00:14,938
it's often the left eye

13
00:00:14,938 --> 00:00:16,201
that stays open,

14
00:00:16,221 --> 00:00:17,293
wired to the half that's

15
00:00:17,293 --> 00:00:17,878
still awake.

16
00:00:18,879 --> 00:00:20,301
So why sleep in halves?

17
00:00:20,321 --> 00:00:21,981
The leading idea is breathing.

18
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:23,281
You breathe automatically,

19
00:00:23,301 --> 00:00:24,605
even in deep sleep.

20
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:25,638
Dolphins are voluntary

21
00:00:25,638 --> 00:00:26,864
breathers: each breath is

22
00:00:26,864 --> 00:00:27,615
something they actively do,

23
00:00:27,635 --> 00:00:29,095
at the surface,

24
00:00:29,115 --> 00:00:30,218
through the blowhole on top

25
00:00:30,218 --> 00:00:30,947
of their head.

26
00:00:30,967 --> 00:00:32,177
Keeping half the brain awake

27
00:00:32,177 --> 00:00:33,312
lets them keep coming up

28
00:00:33,312 --> 00:00:34,909
for that next breath.

29
00:00:34,929 --> 00:00:36,625
Subscribe for more what ifs!
